Context: this is fragment 91 of Euler's Adversaria (Analysis diophantea; the next page cites A. m. T. I p. 17-21). On the previous page an unspecified Diophantine problem has been reduced to the following. Find p, q, r, s, with r = s in the special case, such that λ times a rational function built from p²+s², q²+r² and the four linear combinations p±s, q±r is a square. One of the conditions there was p = c²+d². The problem itself is not stated on this page or its neighbours, so I do not try to identify it.

First part (an 'alius modus'). Euler factorises the four combinations multiplicatively:
- p+s = αβ, p−s = εζ, q+r = αγ, q−r = εη;
- he also imposes q = βζ.

The condition r = s gives α(γ−β) = ε(η−ζ). He takes α = η−ζ and ε = γ−β. The definition of q gives 2βζ = αγ+εη = 2γη − γζ − βη, hence β:γ = (2η−ζ):(2ζ+η). He then sets β = (2η−ζ)/5, γ = (2ζ+η)/5, ε = (3ζ−η)/5. This yields:
- p = (2ζ²−2ζη+η²)/5 (the printed text has 7ζ²; see errors);
- q = ζ(2η−ζ)/5;
- r = s = (η²−ηζ−ζ²)/5;
- p²+s² = (5ζ²−6ζη+2η²)(ζ²+η²)/25;
- q²+r² = (2ζ²−2ζη+η²)(ζ²+η²)/25.
In the last line the first factor is exactly 5p, which is why the earlier solution re-emerges. Euler remarks that only the ratios α:ε, β:γ and ζ:η matter, i.e. the parametrisation is projective.

'Solutio generalior'. Keep the same factorisation but relax r = s to r:s = f:g, and set q = hβζ. The proportion gives α(fβ−gγ) = ε(fζ−gη), so α = fζ−gη and ε = fβ−gγ. The definition of q gives β(2hζ−fη) = γ(fζ−2gη), so β = fζ−2gη and γ = 2hζ−fη. Then ε = (f²−2gh)ζ − fgη. The resulting quadratic forms are:
- p = (f²−gh)ζ² − 2fgζη + g²η²;
- s = g(hζ²−fζη+gη²);
- q = hζ(fζ−2gη);
- r = f(hζ²−fζη+gη²).

Euler expands p²+s² and q²+r² as binary quartics. He then asks when q²+r² is divisible by p. The two conditions are f²+gh = 0 and −3f⁴+f²gh+4g²h² = (f²+gh)(4gh−3f²) = 0, so h = −f²/g suffices for both. The quotient (f²η² + (3f²h/g+4h²)ζ²) then becomes f²η²+h²ζ².

On the next page, with gh = −f², p²+s² = (f²ζ²+g²η²)(5f²ζ²−6fgζη+2g²η²). The two quadratic factors f²ζ²+g²η² and f²η²+h²ζ² coalesce, since f²:h² = g²:f². The condition therefore reduces to λ(5f²ζ²−6fgζη+2g²η²)/(gη(2fζ+gη)) = □. Euler observes this is the earlier solution with ζ, η replaced by fζ, gη. The 'generalisation' is thus illusory: it is the special solution after a rescaling of variables. Taking f = g = 1, h = −1 recovers the first part exactly.

Modern view: Euler parametrises a quadric-type system by factoring sums and differences, a standard 'composition by factors' trick. He uses a divisibility/resultant condition between binary forms to cancel common factors, leaving a single quartic λ·Q(ζ,η)·L₁L₂ = □. That is a genus-1 (quartic twist) condition; Euler leaves it unsolved here.
